Right, the workflow for discovering certificates:
- Walk [known-paths](https://github.com/facebook/osquery/blob/master/osquery/tables/system/darwin/keychain_utils.cpp#L24) to user and system keychains [1]
- Open each keychain and walk each certificate item
- Parse the x509x3 details using Apple's SecurityFoundation APIs [2]

[1] There are other variables we could use to detect keychain search paths as well as the user's "default" or "login" keychain. That might save time.
[2] These APIs also leak memory when parsing ASN.1 OIDs. :(

I haven't explored if the latency comes from walking the FS search paths, enumerating the keychain items, or parsing certificate blobs. If the parsing is latent (it's definitely leaky) that's more motivation to move to openssl-based parsing. 
If I had to speculate, I'd say that OpenSSL is pretty slow. For comparison, here's a small go program:

``` go
package main

import (
    "crypto/x509"
    "fmt"
    "os/exec"
)

func appendFromFile(roots *x509.CertPool, f string) {
    cmd := exec.Command(
        "/usr/bin/security", "find-certificate", "-a", "-p", f,
    )
    data, err := cmd.Output()
    if err != nil {
        panic(err)
    }

    roots.AppendCertsFromPEM(data)
}

func main() {
    roots := x509.NewCertPool()

    appendFromFile(
        roots, "/System/Library/Keychains/SystemRootCertificates.keychain",
    )
    appendFromFile(
        roots, "/System/Library/Keychains/SystemCACertificates.keychain",
    )
    appendFromFile(roots, "/Library/Keychains/System.keychain")

    fmt.Println(len(roots.Subjects()))
}
```

If you build that and then run it, it takes about 100ms.
Err, that should be "CommonCrypto" (or maybe libsecurity? I don't really understand how Apple's chosen to lay out these libraries)
Right, that's CommonCrypto, the same APIs we're currently using. :) OpenSSL on OS X is "deprecated" but you still get a shared lib in /usr/lib and there's no clue on when it'll disappear. I don't mind too-much using it for 509x3 parsing though it's not ideal. Another ASN.1 parser from boost's projects may work too.
To be clear, the Go example uses Go's included (pure Go) X509 parser.

FWIW I'm not sure if OpenSSL can parse the `.keychain` files directly, it dosen't look like they're ASN.1 or any other format I'm familiar with; my example uses the `security` tool to conver them to PEM before parsing.
Gotya on the x509 parsing, I saw the call to `/usr/bin/security` and jumped to conclusions. 

We'll need to stick with `SecurityFoundation` to access the keychain containers. There's quite a bit of work done to decrypt, manage access, and parse the contents. But we might be able to speed up the blob/ASN.1 data returned. I wonder if you can change the go example to parse a single certificate multiple times and remove any exec/foundation overhead. 
I'll try to get some ObjC/C++ benchmarks going tonight to run this down, here's a basic/isolated walk of the certs (selecting only the authorities) in the default keychain:

``` cpp
#include <CoreFoundation/CoreFoundation.h>
#include <CoreFoundation/CFData.h>
#include <Security/Security.h>

void CreateAuthorities() {
    CFMutableDictionaryRef query;
    query = CFDictionaryCreateMutable(nullptr,
                                      0,
                                      &kCFTypeDictionaryKeyCallBacks,
                                      &kCFTypeDictionaryValueCallBacks);
    CFDictionaryAddValue(query, kSecClass, kSecClassCertificate);
    CFDictionaryAddValue(query, kSecReturnRef, kCFBooleanTrue);
    // This can be added to restrict results to x509v3
    CFDictionaryAddValue(query, kSecAttrCanVerify, kCFBooleanTrue);
    CFDictionaryAddValue(query, kSecMatchLimit, kSecMatchLimitAll);

    CFArrayRef keychain_certs;
    auto status = SecItemCopyMatching(query, (CFTypeRef *)&keychain_certs);
    CFRelease(query);

    if (status != errSecSuccess) {
        return;
    }

    // Store just the authority certificates.
    auto authorities = CFArrayCreateMutable(nullptr, 0, &kCFTypeArrayCallBacks);

    // For each certificate returned from the search, get the constraints prop.
    for (CFIndex i = 0; i < CFArrayGetCount(keychain_certs); i++) {
        auto cert = (SecCertificateRef)CFArrayGetValueAtIndex(keychain_certs, i);

        auto keys = CFArrayCreateMutable(nullptr, 0, &kCFTypeArrayCallBacks);
        CFArrayAppendValue(keys, kSecOIDBasicConstraints); // SecCertificateOIDs.h

        // Request dictionary of dictionaries (one for each attribute).
        auto certificate_values = SecCertificateCopyValues(cert, keys, nullptr);
        CFRelease(keys);

        // Assume some checking to make sure constraints include CA: true.
        CFRelease(certificate_values);
        // Then if constraint exists, append.
        CFArrayAppendValue(authorities, cert);
    }

    CFRelease(keychain_certs);
    // Return this authorities list.
    CFRelease(authorities);
    return;
}

int main_certificates(int argc, const char * argv[]) {
    CreateAuthorities();
}
```
